# The last statement keeps the prototype property from being proven: a proven one is not read at the construction.
# Once the guards of a constructor function have begun, the walk assumes that the read of its prototype ends no guard that held.
# The run-time effects check (testing=CheckEffectsAtRunTime) checks the operation against that assumption, by the count of guards that ended.
# The count of guards that were armed is left unchecked for this operation, by name, because that very read may define the class.
# Here the first object is allocated before the statements that set up the class, so the class is first encountered at that read.
# Without the fault, the guards have not begun there, the walk assumes nothing about the read, and the effects check reports nothing.
# Fault injection, testing=IgnorePrototypeReads: the walk assumes that the guards have begun. The effects check fires, so the count of guards that ended is still checked for this operation.
# This does not show a guard that held before the call and then ended: the runtime also counts the guards that it arms and ends within one call.
# If a program ever triggers this report without an injected fault, look into that first.
# The test stores no run with the fault injected and without the effects check.
